Good afternoon Kalin,I will try to answer your questions first, since muc=
h of the conditions of the lot is out of our scope.

Regarding the plat, please reach out to Durham County Tax Land Records to=
 see why the plat does not include the south western portion that shows u=
p on our GIS maps. They are the folks that put together all parcel bounda=
ries that we have available: Tax-MappingGIS@dconc.gov

All records of the landfill are held by NCDWM. Any specific question abou=
t their findings should be directed to them.
See below for development standards
See below for contacts that can assist with your questions about the floo=
d and wetland areas
See below for a contact for the Building &amp; Safety Department who can =
assist with your questions about building permits, liens, and impact fees=
.



Please note there is a notice that this property was used as an unpermitt=
ed solid waste disposal site. Please see attached. Any additional regulat=
ions on development may be in the hands of the State. Please reach out to=
 the NC Department of Waste Management =E2=80=93 Solid Waste Section to s=
ee what requirements they have regarding development, if there are any. B=
elow are a few numbers I was able to track down:General Waste Management =
Inquiries: 919-707-8200
Anna Mullen =E2=80=93 Solid Waste: 919-707-8281
Tim Davis =E2=80=93 Solid Waste: 919-707-8290You may also want to reach o=
ut to our Environmental and Street Services team to see if the City has a=
nything to recommend regarding developing this property. Please contact M=
ichelle Woolfolk:&nbsp;Michelle.Woolfolk@durhamnc.gov&nbsp;

You may need to reach out to the Building &amp; Safety Department, as I b=
elieve there may need to be soil test if you plan to do development on th=
e property to check for any compaction issues due the nature of the prope=
rty's previous use as a landfill. Please contact them here:&nbsp;PermitTe=
chnicians@durhamnc.gov&nbsp;

The property is located in the F/J-B watershed protection overlay. You wi=
ll need to find when the property was platted via deed in order to find o=
ut what impervious surface limits the property will be subject to per&nbs=
p;8.7.3B.&nbsp;There are streams on the property, which are perennial str=
eams. These will have 100' undisturbed vegetative buffers at the top of e=
ach bank, plus additional 10' no build no grade buffers on top of that. S=
ee attached for approximate location of stream (purple).
8.5. Riparian Buffer Protection Standards -https://udo.durhamnc.gov/udo/8=
05Riparian%20Buffer%20Protection.htm#8.5.&nbsp;There are also flood areas=
 on the property, both flood hazard areas and a floodway. See attached fi=
le (floodway =3D blue hashed lines). Only FEMA approval can grant develop=
ment within the floodway. Please see UDO 8.4.3E for information regarding=
 development within the flood way.&nbsp;
Please reach out to&nbsp;DSCFloodplain@durhamnc.gov, our Floodplain team,=
 to discuss what the development requirements are for developing in the f=
lood hazard area.
8.4. Floodplain and Flood Damage Protection Standards -&nbsp;https://udo.=
durhamnc.gov/udo/804Floodplain%20and%20Flood%20Damage.htm#8.4.&nbsp;
A portion of the property is also location in the National Wetland Invent=
ory. Please reach out to our Stormwater team here for any questions on th=
is:&nbsp;StormwaterBMPs@durhamnc.gov&nbsp;This property is zoned RS-10, s=
uburban tier. =E2=80=9CRS=E2=80=9D stands for Residential Suburban and pe=
rmits primarily residential uses. You can look at our full use table in 5=
.1.2 here:&nbsp;https://udo.durhamnc.gov/udo/501Use%20Table.htm#5.1.2&nbs=
p;
=E2=80=9CP=E2=80=9D means yes, any other letter means yet with limitation=
s, and a blank space means no.
